/_docs_toto
/_temp
/build-check

# Python bytecode from the static checks in tests/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# gfortran's fallback output. A WRITE to a unit that was never successfully
# OPENed lands in fort.<unit> in the working directory, so a failed run leaves
# stray output here that looks like a source change. The unit number is picked
# from the clock at startup, so the names vary from run to run.
fort.*
/smartflux_example
/.moc
/.obj
#> `/tests` used to be listed here, among the build directories, and it was
#> doing nothing but harm. Every one of the 63 files under tests/ is tracked
#> anyway - git ignores only files it does not already know - so the line
#> hid exactly one category: static checks added AFTER it. A new
#> test_*_static.py was invisible to `git status`, so it ran locally, passed,
#> and was never committed. The Python bytecode it was presumably meant to
#> catch is already covered by the __pycache__/ and *.pyc rules above.

# qmake/make build output - regenerated by qmake + mingw32-make, see
# eddyflow-build-toolchain. Makefile.Debug/.Release are qmake's per-config
# rules; plain Makefile picks between them. .res/ is windres output, the
# two .rc files are generated from the .qrc resources at build time, and
# debug/release/ hold the linked binaries themselves.
/.qmake.stash
/.res
/Makefile
/Makefile.Debug
/Makefile.Release
/EddyFlow_resource.rc
/EddyFlow_debug_resource.rc
/debug
/release
